      <div class="prose">
        <p>Remote work sold everyone a promise: live where you want, keep the job. The catch nobody mentioned is that where you live has to actually support the work. A fast connection, a place that isn't your kitchen, and a day that doesn't dissolve into laundry and loneliness. Setúbal, 35 minutes south of Lisboa, turns out to be very good at exactly that.</p>

        <p>This is the practical guide, from people who do it here every day.</p>

        <h2>Why Setúbal works for remote workers</h2>
        <p>You stay close to Lisboa without paying to live in it. The airport, the client meeting, the occasional office day are all within reach, but your rent, your coffee and your parking all cost less. In exchange you get the Arrábida hills, a walkable centre and the coast. For someone whose office is a laptop, that swap is hard to beat.</p>

        <p>The other thing that matters: Setúbal is filling up with people doing the same. Founders, freelancers, remote employees. That means the infrastructure and the company are already here, which is the difference between working remotely and just working alone.</p>

        <h2>The setup that actually matters</h2>
        <p><strong>Internet you can trust.</strong> Home fibre in Setúbal is fine until it isn't, and a dropped call in front of a client is expensive. The fix is having a reliable fallback you don't manage yourself. At <a href="/#spec">22cowork we run a 10&nbsp;Gbps fibre backbone with WiFi&nbsp;7</a>, so calls and big uploads are never the thing that goes wrong.</p>

        <p><strong>A place that isn't home.</strong> Working from the sofa is a slow tax on your focus and your evenings, because work never quite ends. A desk you leave at the end of the day gives you back the boundary remote work quietly took away. If you want the full argument, we wrote about <a href="/blog/deep-focus-by-design">why focus is a design problem, not a willpower one</a>.</p>

        <p><strong>A room for calls.</strong> Remote work is calls. You need somewhere you can take three in a row without apologising for the background, and ideally a closed studio if you record anything.</p>

        <p><strong>The boring logistics.</strong> Parking, coffee, a door that locks. None of it is glamorous, and all of it decides whether the day feels easy or like friction from 8am.</p>

        <blockquote>Remote work doesn't fail on the big things. It fails on the small ones, repeated every day.</blockquote>

        <h2>A week working remotely from Setúbal</h2>
        <p>The rhythm is the point. Park underground, first coffee, and you're at your desk by nine with the connection just working. Deep work in the morning while the room is quiet. A call room when you need it. A walk by the water at lunch because it's there. And on the days you don't need the desk at all, you don't come, that's the freedom you moved here for. Most people find that having a base, even a few days a week, is what makes the other days feel like a choice rather than isolation.</p>

        <h2>What it costs, honestly</h2>
        <p>Less than Lisboa, on almost every line. Housing, workspace and daily life are all cheaper on the peninsula, so the same income stretches noticeably further. A dedicated desk costs less than most people expect, and a good deal less than the productivity you lose fighting a home setup. The real saving, though, is the commute you no longer make and the evenings you get back.</p>

        <h2>Making it official</h2>
        <p>If you freelance or run a small company, your home address doesn't belong on your invoices. A <a href="/blog/virtual-office-setubal">real Setúbal business address</a> separates work from home cleanly and looks the part to clients. It's a small thing that quietly makes you look more established than a home postcode ever will.</p>

        <h2>The honest trade-off</h2>
        <p>Setúbal isn't Lisboa, and that's the entire appeal. You give up some of the density, the constant events, the being-in-the-middle-of-it. You get space, quiet, coast and money back. For focused remote work, that's not a compromise, it's the upgrade. If you're weighing the move, the <a href="/blog/setubal-tech-hub">wider case for building here</a> is worth a read too.</p>

        <h2>Try it before you commit</h2>
        <p>You'll know within a day whether working here fits your week. That's genuinely the best way to decide, better than any guide, this one included.</p>
